---
import ModellingLayout from '@/layouts/ModellingLayout.astro';
import LearningIllustration from '@/components/modelling/LearningIllustration.astro';
import LearningCard from '@/components/modelling/LearningCard.astro';
import Callout from '@/components/Callout.astro';
---

<ModellingLayout
  title="Benefits, limits and reasonable objections"
  description="Shared meaning is worth the effort when it preserves distinctions people need. Examine the benefit, the conditions and the work that remains."
  proseClass="prose wide modelling-document learning-document"
>
  <section aria-labelledby="what-improves">
    <h2 id="what-improves">Start with the misunderstanding you want to prevent</h2>
    <p>
      A connected model is not valuable simply because it contains many connections.
      It is valuable when people can answer a practical question with less guessing:
      which dwelling does this account concern, what does this amount represent, or
      which version supports this statement? The improvement is a clearer agreement
      about those questions, not a promise that every process will become cheaper or
      every piece of information more accurate.
    </p>
    <p>
      Consider two fictional floor-area accounts for the same dwelling. Account A
      gives 68 m², excluding an enclosed 4 m² area. Account B gives 72 m², including
      it. These premises explain the difference without choosing a professional
      measuring standard. Now consider what happens when the accounts are exchanged.
    </p>
    <div class="learning-comparison">
      <LearningCard labelledby="distinction-lost">
        <h3 id="distinction-lost">Exchange A · The basis is lost</h3>
        <dl>
          <dt>What arrives</dt><dd>Area: 68 m². Other area: 72 m².</dd>
          <dt>What the recipient can conclude</dt><dd>Two different numbers were supplied. Their comparability is not established.</dd>
          <dt>Risk</dt><dd>A recipient may treat them as incompatible measurements of the same area, or silently keep the larger value.</dd>
        </dl>
      </LearningCard>
      <LearningCard labelledby="distinction-kept">
        <h3 id="distinction-kept">Exchange B · The basis survives</h3>
        <dl>
          <dt>What arrives</dt><dd>The same dwelling; 68 m² excluding the enclosed 4 m² area; 72 m² including it; separate references to accounts A and B.</dd>
          <dt>What the recipient can conclude</dt><dd>The stated inclusion difference explains the four-square-metre difference in this example.</dd>
          <dt>Remaining question</dt><dd>Which basis is appropriate for the intended use? The exchange does not decide that.</dd>
        </dl>
      </LearningCard>
    </div>
    <p>
      The second exchange supports a better explanation because it preserves subject,
      unit, basis and source—not because it uses a particular file format. The
      <a href="/semantic-modelling/explore/measurements-amounts-and-values">measurements chapter</a>
      develops this example, including a harder case where the missing evidence
      prevents the same conclusion.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="benefits-with-conditions">
    <h2 id="benefits-with-conditions">Five benefits, with their conditions attached</h2>
    <h3>Fewer repeated reinterpretations</h3>
    <p>
      If sender and recipient use the same understood distinction, a person should
      not need to explain it afresh at every handoff. The condition is that the
      distinction survives the exchange and the recipient uses it correctly.
      If “measured area” is flattened back into one unexplained field, a carefully
      defined model upstream will not rescue the lost meaning downstream.
    </p>
    <h3>Evidence that is easier to inspect</h3>
    <p>
      A statement linked to its source and version gives a reviewer somewhere useful
      to look. That requires maintained references, relevant passages and an honest
      account of what the source supports. A link to an entire document, or to an
      inaccessible version without an interpretable description, may leave the
      important question unanswered. Provenance makes investigation possible; it is
      not a certificate of truth.
    </p>
    <h3>Agreements that practitioners can challenge</h3>
    <p>
      A readable definition, example or relationship lets somebody say precisely
      what is wrong. That benefit depends on reviewable wording and a reasoned human
      response. If an essential exclusion exists only in code, a domain expert may
      never see it. If a polished diagram conceals an unresolved assumption, visual
      clarity can create false confidence rather than understanding.
    </p>
    <h3>Deliberate reuse</h3>
    <p>
      A well-defined concept can be reused where its meaning genuinely fits. Reuse
      requires comparing scope, identity, purpose and relevant constraints—not just
      matching labels. Reusing a term that almost fits can hide a consequential
      difference. Sometimes a qualified correspondence between distinct meanings is
      the useful result; merging them is not always an improvement.
    </p>
    <LearningIllustration scene="context-bridges" alt="Watercolour of two differently coloured neighbourhoods connected by a bridge, a metaphor for useful connections that preserve distinct meanings." />
    <h3>A clearer account of change</h3>
    <p>
      If a definition or source changes, explicit connections help identify which
      interpretations need review and which can stay the same. This requires version
      information and an explanation of the change. Overwriting the previous
      definition removes the very evidence needed to explain why an earlier decision
      used it. Conversely, preserving a version does not establish that it remains
      appropriate for current use.
    </p>
    <p class="learning-note">These are intended benefits and ways to test them, not measured performance claims for the current Property Pack candidate. No time-saving percentage or commercial return is implied.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="the-cost">
    <h2 id="the-cost">What the approach asks of people</h2>
    <p>
      Definitions need owners and maintenance. Difficult cases need discussion.
      Sources need enough context to be interpretable and enough permission to be
      used. Boundary agreements need representatives who understand both sides.
      Generated representations need checks that they preserve the meaning and the
      constraints they claim to carry. These are real costs, not work that vanishes
      because an ontology exists.
    </p>
    <p>
      Bounded work helps make those costs manageable. A group can address a specific
      question inside its documented scope, reuse an established meaning where it
      fits and record a justified exclusion. It need not model every possible property
      situation before producing something useful. But a smaller scope must remain
      visible: an agreement for one purpose should not quietly become a rule for all
      purposes.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="reasonable-objections">
    <h2 id="reasonable-objections">Reasonable objections deserve more than slogans</h2>
    <h3 id="already-a-schema">“We already have a schema.”</h3>
    <p>
      A schema is valuable. It can describe a document's structure and constrain
      permitted content; its accompanying documentation may contain substantial
      domain knowledge. The question is whether those materials make the necessary
      subjects, relationships and conditions explicit across uses. A well-formed
      field called “date” still needs a meaning.
    </p>
    <p>
      OPDA's earlier PDTF schema and its separately identified schema-derived ontology
      remain useful evidence for requirements, traceability and future migration
      analysis. The recorded <a href="/semantic-modelling/decision-basis">decision basis</a>
      does not treat either as an OPDA-endorsed predecessor scheme or as automatic
      authority for future domain meaning. The approach changes how that meaning is
      established; it does not discard everything learned from the existing material.
    </p>
    <h3 id="one-definition">“Why not agree one definition of property?”</h3>
    <p>
      Shared definitions help when the intended meaning really is shared. But a
      building, a dwelling, a right or interest described by a title record, and a
      subject considered in a lending decision are not made identical by calling
      them “property”. OPDA works within documented contexts so useful local
      distinctions remain explicit. At their boundaries, people must establish what
      information another context needs and what a proposed connection preserves.
      This is not permission for arbitrary disagreement; it is a way to locate and
      examine the difference.
    </p>
    <h3 id="existing-standards">“Why not import an existing ontology such as FIBO?”</h3>
    <p>
      Reusing established work can avoid reinvention. ADR-0063 identifies the
      Financial Industry Business Ontology as a useful reference and potential source
      of concepts for Finance and Banking. It also says the group should establish
      its scope and questions before considering wholesale import. A term's existence
      in another ontology does not demonstrate that it answers this group's question
      with the right scope. Other modelling approaches also support evidence and
      participation; OPDA's choice is not a claim that all alternatives impose one
      top-down model.
    </p>
    <h3 id="identifiers">“Is an identifier enough?”</h3>
    <p>
      An identifier helps us refer to something consistently. It cannot establish
      that two organisations assigned it to the same subject, or decide what happens
      when a dwelling is subdivided. Review the identity criterion and the evidence
      for applying it. The identifier supports the agreement; it does not replace it.
    </p>
    <h3 id="forms-and-systems">“Will this replace our forms or require one database?”</h3>
    <p>
      Not inherently. Forms and systems can offer useful views of a shared agreement,
      and organisations need not centralise all their records to share meaning.
      Implementation and migration still require separate choices and testing.
      A model does not automatically update an existing form, translate a historic
      field correctly or make every recipient adopt a new exchange.
    </p>
    <h3 id="ai">“Is AI deciding the standard?”</h3>
    <p>
      No. AI can help examine evidence and draft candidates. Its proposals may omit
      context, inherit a source's mistake or produce plausible wording unsupported
      by evidence. Practitioners judge domain meaning; technical checks assess
      specified conformance; recorded OPDA authority governs promotion and release.
      Agreement between models does not substitute for those responsibilities.
    </p>
    <h3 id="restricted-evidence">“Can we contribute when the evidence is restricted?”</h3>
    <p>
      Often a bounded description of the question, an authorised excerpt or a
      carefully labelled fictional example can help. Describe the source's relevance
      and restriction without copying material you may not share. A fictional example
      can reveal an ambiguity, but it is not proof of a claim about real practice.
      The <a href="/development/working-groups/member-guide">member guide</a> is the
      home for current participation and evidence-handling instructions.
    </p>
  </section>

  <Callout title="What a model cannot guarantee" tone="important" heading="h2" id="accuracy-and-access">
    <p>
      An accurate definition cannot make an inaccurate source true. A passing data
      check cannot establish that an inspection happened. A source reference cannot
      guarantee that its contents remain current. A description of permission does
      not authenticate somebody or grant access to records. A reviewable candidate
      does not become an approved release or acquire external recognition because
      it appears on a website.
    </p>
    <p>
      These distinctions do not weaken the case for modelling. They show where the
      model helps and where other evidence or action is still required. The right
      claim is “this makes the meaning and its limitations inspectable”, not “this
      removes every risk in property-data exchange”.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <section class="learning-example document-flow" aria-labelledby="test-the-benefit">
    <h2 id="test-the-benefit">Practice · Put a condition on the claimed benefit</h2>
    <p>
      A project note says: “Once both area accounts use our model, recipients will
      no longer have to investigate different floor areas.” Use the opening exchanges
      to write a more defensible claim. What work remains even in exchange B?
    </p>
    <h3>Worked response</h3>
    <p>
      “If both accounts identify the same dwelling and preserve their values, units,
      inclusion bases and sources, a recipient can explain this difference without
      guessing what each area includes.” The evidence supports that narrower benefit.
      It does not select an appropriate measuring basis for a real purpose, verify
      the underlying measurements or ensure that a receiving system will display
      the explanation correctly.
    </p>
    <h3>A harder variation</h3>
    <p>
      Suppose both accounts now claim the same inclusion basis but still give 68 m²
      and 72 m². Retaining the basis remains useful: it shows that the earlier
      explanation no longer resolves the difference. Ask for the relevant measuring
      methods, dates and source evidence. A good model can help locate a disagreement
      without resolving it. State that limit alongside the benefit you advocate.
    </p>
  </section>
</ModellingLayout>
